using System;
using System.IO;

public static void CopyFiles(string sourceDir, string targetDir, params string[] fileExtensions)
{
    if (!Directory.Exists(sourceDir))
    {
        throw new DirectoryNotFoundException($"Source directory '{sourceDir}' does not exist.");
    }

    if (Directory.Exists(targetDir))
    {
        throw new IOException($"Target directory '{targetDir}' already exists.");
    }

    // Create the target directory if it doesn't exist
    Directory.CreateDirectory(targetDir);

    foreach (string file in Directory.GetFiles(sourceDir, "*", SearchOption.AllDirectories))
    {
        string fileName = Path.GetFileName(file);
        string extension = Path.GetExtension(fileName).ToLower();

        if (!fileExtensions.Contains(extension))
        {
            continue;
        }

        // Copy the file to the target directory
        File.Copy(file, Path.Combine(targetDir, fileName), true);
    }
}
```
This method takes three arguments:

* `sourceDir`: The path of the source directory where the files are located.
* `targetDir`: The path of the target directory where the files should be copied to.
* `fileExtensions`: An array of file extensions that should be included in the copy operation. Files with any other extension will be excluded from the copy operation.

The method first checks if the source and target directories exist, and throws an exception if either one does not exist. It then creates the target directory if it doesn't already exist.

Next, it uses `Directory.GetFiles()` to get a list of all files in the source directory and its subdirectories, using the `SearchOption.AllDirectories` option to include files in subdirectories as well. For each file, it checks the extension of the file against the list of file extensions provided as an argument. If the extension is not in the list, it skips the file and moves on to the next one.

If the extension is in the list, the method copies the file to the target directory using `File.Copy()`. The `true` parameter at the end of the `File.Copy()` method call tells it to overwrite any existing files in the target directory with the same name as the source file.

Note that this code assumes that the source and target directories are both located on the local machine, and that they can be accessed using the `Directory` and `File` classes provided by .NET. If you need to copy files from a remote location or use a different approach for accessing the filesystem, you will need to modify the code accordingly.

