CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION limit_query (sql text, offset integer, limit integer) RETURNS text AS $$
DECLARE 
    query text;
BEGIN
    -- some db's require limit to be present of offset is
    IF offset IS NOT NULL AND limit IS NULL THEN
        SET limit = Integer.MAX_VALUE; -- ensure we don't wrap around
    END IF;
    IF limit IS NOT NULL AND offset IS NULL THEN
        SET offset = 0;
        SET limit += 1; -- not zero-based
    END IF;
    IF offset IS NOT NULL AND limit IS NOT NULL THEN
        query := 'SELECT * FROM (SELECT query.*, rownum rnum FROM (' || sql || ') query';
        IF limit <> Integer.MAX_VALUE THEN
            query := query || ' WHERE rownum <= ' || limit;
        END IF;
        query := query || ' WHERE rnum > ' || offset;
    END IF;
    RETURN query;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
```
This function accepts three parameters: `sql`, `offset`, and `limit`. It uses the `DECLARE` keyword to declare a variable called `query` of type `text`. Then, it checks if both `offset` and `limit` are not null and sets them accordingly. If only one of them is null, it sets the other one based on the value of the non-null parameter. Finally, it concatenates the SQL query with the necessary conditions to limit the number of rows returned, using the `WHERE` clause to filter out unwanted rows. The function then returns the modified SQL query as a text string.

