The function opens a log file for writing using `fopen` and checks if it was successful. If not, it throws an exception. It then uses `proc_open` to execute the command and captures its exit status with `proc_get_status`. The output of the process is written to the log file using `fwrite`, and the function returns the output when the process exits successfully (i.e., `$exitcode === 0`). If the process fails, it throws an exception with the error message.

Note that this function uses `proc_open` instead of `shell_exec`, which allows for more control over the execution environment and the ability to capture the output in real-time. Additionally, the command is escaped before being executed to prevent shell injection attacks.

